## internal/bbr

The BBR congestion controller, from the core/internal/congestion directory of Hysteria
(https://github.com/apernet/hysteria, repository head c2b181e0b0e72b077e4b0b7297735af14d1f9929, the directory last
changed in commit 93f68c526b2bf5b45ac7d8577698aa754b699ffc). Changed here: the tests, the debug output, the profile
parsing, the conservative and aggressive profiles with the options only they turned on (overestimate avoidance, drain to
target, overshoot detection, ack aggregation in start-up) and the helper functions that nothing calls are removed, a span cap
on the packet-number queue is added (see docs/limits.md), the rate arithmetic cannot overflow, the pacer is part of the package, imports point to
internal/quicgo, a datagram size that shrinks is ignored instead of a panic, and the pacer starts with the connection's
first datagram size. The license file is internal/bbr/LICENSE.md.

The code in internal/bbr has three origins, and a license applies to each:

- Hysteria itself, under the MIT license below ("Copyright 2023 Toby").
- bbr_sender.go, bandwidth_sampler.go, bandwidth.go and windowed_filter.go are a port of Google's QUIC BBR (the QUICHE
  sources, formerly in Chromium). Their comments keep the names of that code (`kStartupGrowthTarget`,
  `quic_bbr_cwnd_gain`, `QuicBandwidth::Zero()`, "Kathleen Nichols' algorithm"). The files in Hysteria carry no header, so
  the port is known from these comments and not from a statement of Hysteria; the matching QUICHE files (for example
  bbr_sender.cc) carry "Copyright 2016 The Chromium Authors" and refer to the BSD-style license that follows, so that
  notice is kept here.
- ringbuffer.go and pacer.go follow files of quic-go (internal/utils/ringbuffer and internal/congestion/pacer.go), which
  are under the quic-go license in the internal/quicgo section above.

